---
// Pearl › 05 — Building the Engine (dark band, the centerpiece): badge, label and centred
// headline, two paragraphs, a three-stat row, then the focus-state paragraph and two
// sub-points ("The harder calls", "The harder test") in the reading column.
//
// The band is wide so the stats can span 972; the text is held to the 612 column inside it.
// The nav goes white while this band is behind it (Section tone="dark").
import Section from './Section.astro';

const stats = [
  { value: '8', label: 'Core variants' },
  { value: '90', label: 'Generated' },
  { value: '1', label: 'Edit point' },
];
---

<Section tone="dark" width="wide" labelledby="engine-title">
  <div class="column">
    <div class="heading">
      <span class="text-title-l badge">Centerpiece</span>
      <p class="text-label-m label">Building the engine</p>
      <h2 id="engine-title">
        One base component, <span class="accent">ninety variants,</span> one edit point
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="lead text-body-xl">
      <p>
        The audit had already shown what inconsistency have cost. The old design ran two
        competing input field styles side by side, and buttons varied in size across scenarios
        that should have matched. Every major component in Pearl started as a single base
        instead, built with boolean properties rather than duplicated instances. The input field
        is the clearest example — one base component with 8 core variants generated roughly 90
        downstream variants, covering every style and state combination.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body-l-mobile">
        That structure paid for itself the first time it mattered. Mid-project, the client asked
        to move the input label from inside the field to sitting on the border. Instead of
        updating 90 variants individually, I changed the base component once. Everything
        downstream updated with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="stats">
    {stats.map(({ value, label }) => (
      <div class="stat">
        <dt class="text-label-m">{label}</dt>
        <dd class="text-number-l">{value}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="column">
    <p class="text-body-xl text-body-l-mobile focus">
      The same logic shaped harder calls, like focus states. Mobile and web needed different
      treatments — a border adjustment on mobile, a visible ring on web, a specific fix where a
      ring was causing layout shift on forms — and disabled buttons needed their own separate
      reasoning about whether a focus state made sense at all. Each was checked against how
      Material, Atlassian, and eBay had solved the same problem, before deciding what Pearl's
      version should do.
    </p>

    <div class="points">
      <section class="point" aria-labelledby="engine-calls">
        <h3 class="text-title-m point-title" id="engine-calls">The harder calls</h3>
        <div class="pair text-body-xl text-body-l-mobile">
          <p>
            Focus itself needed correcting once. Buttons initially treated it as a plain state,
            until it became clear a user could hover or press at the same time — so focus became
            its own property instead, independent of the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            Disabled buttons split into two cases. A hard-disabled button loses focus outright,
            the browser kills it. A soft-disabled one — still showing a tooltip on hover — hadn't
            actually lost it, so it kept its ring.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="point" aria-labelledby="engine-test">
        <h3 class="text-title-m point-title" id="engine-test">The harder test</h3>
        <p class="text-body-xl text-body-l-mobile">
          The list component was the hardest test of the model. Duplicating mobile's variants
          into web didn't hold — the structure broke down, so the rebuild started over from just
          the base component, updating slot instances once instead. A second near-miss followed:
          focus states were about to be duplicated across every named list type before realizing
          that would double the maintenance problem all over again. Focus moved into the base
          instead, the same discipline the rest of the system already relied on.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  /* Narrow reading column (612) centred in the wide band. */
  .column {
    max-width: 612px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  /* Figma gaps 20, snapped to 24. */
  .heading {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .badge {
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-12);
    border: 1px solid var(--pearl-badge-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--pearl-badge-bg);
    color: var(--pearl-badge-text);
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--text-white);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .accent {
    color: var(--text-pearl-secondary);
  }

  /* Figma gap 18, snapped to 16. */
  .lead {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    margin-top: var(--space-48);
  }

  /* No card on the dark band: three cells across the 972 column, 48 below the text. */
  .stats {
    display: flex;
    margin: var(--space-48) 0 0;
  }

  /* Label sits under the number visually; dt comes first for screen readers. */
  .stat {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column-reverse;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    padding: var(--space-40) var(--space-32);
  }

  dt {
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-pearl);
  }

  .focus {
    margin-top: var(--space-80);
  }

  .points {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-48);
    margin-top: var(--space-48);
  }

  /* Figma gap 20, snapped to 24. */
  .point {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  /* 6px white dot, 8 before the title (Figma 10, snapped). */
  .point-title {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--text-off-white);
  }

  .point-title::before {
    content: '';
    flex: none;
    width: var(--space-6);
    height: var(--space-6);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--fg-white);
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1199px) {
    .stat {
      padding-inline: var(--space-16);
    }
  }

  /* Mobile: badge, label and headline are left-aligned (user); stats and the paragraph
     pair stack. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .heading {
      align-items: flex-start;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .lead {
      margin-top: var(--space-24);
    }

    .stats {
      flex-direction: column;
      margin-top: var(--space-32);
    }

    .stat {
      gap: var(--space-8);
      padding-block: var(--space-24);
    }

    .focus {
      margin-top: var(--space-48);
    }

    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
